V-Core: Toward Autonomous Scientific Authority

A Type-Theoretic Architecture for Verifiable Machine Science

Version 11.0 — Proof-Carrying Invariants, Lineage-Bounded Authority, and Effective-Time Revocation

---

Abstract

We present Version 11.0 of V-Core, a formal architecture for machine-generated scientific claims that acquire typed authority without per-claim human validation, under stipulated constitutional assumptions. Version 11.0 is a corrective release: it aligns the mechanization with the prose, closing several gaps flagged in review of v10.0. Six substantive changes are made. (i) The proposition parameter T is no longer phantom: Proof is parameterised by an explicit, named set of checkers, and the TCB assumption Sound c is a hypothesis on the mechanized theorem, never an implicit axiom. (ii) Claim acquires a private constructor; its invariants (authority bound, capability membership) are carried as fields, so they survive even if the constructor discipline is relaxed. (iii) Faithfulness is re-formalised as a relation Reform P T populated by an independent formalizer with T \leftrightarrow T' as data, replacing the vacuous Faithfulness.yes = Faithfulness.yes. (iv) No-escalation is proved over a lineage structure Descends a L, mechanically enforcing that a verifier's authority is bounded by every ancestor's, correcting the reversed induction in v10.0. (v) Dependents are defined on a global Ledger with Relation.TransGen, so Lemma 7.5 is genuinely transitive and admits dependents with different propositions. (vi) Revocation is effective-time indexed, so pre-compromise claims survive. The Promotion guard is corrected: a claim is held if a dependency is held, not if a dependent is held. The coverage table is downgraded to match the repository, the export-format risk is documented with named prior art (nanocomparator, FLT's permitted_axioms, unsorry's diff-of-formalisations), and citation errors are corrected. We do not report empirical results.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Verification Problem

We separate capability (producing specialist-quality output) from authority (trust without per-claim human validation). The governing question is unchanged:

Under what conditions can machine-generated scientific claims acquire authority without per-claim human validation, under explicitly stipulated constitutional assumptions?

1.2 What Changed from v10.0

Version 10.0 asserted that a "soundness gap" was closed by parameterising Proof and Claim on T : \mathrm{Prop}. In fact, Proof T carried no evidence of T, because its payload was proofBytes : String. Version 11.0 replaces this with a checker-parameterised proof (Definition 4.2), in which soundness is an explicit hypothesis on any theorem that consumes the proof. Similarly, v10.0's seal returned a Claim type from a public structure; the invariant was true only of the function, not of the type. Version 11.0 uses a private constructor and field-carried invariants so the invariant is a property of the type.

1.3 Contributions

1. Checker-parameterised proofs (§4). The proof-checking TCB is a named hypothesis Sound c, not an implicit assumption. Diversity theorem: one sound checker suffices.
2. Lineage-bounded authority (§5). No-escalation is stated over arbitrary finite chains and proved by structural induction on Descends.
3. Faithfulness as data (§6). Reform P T witnesses an independent formalizer with T \leftrightarrow T'; the negative branch \phi(c) = \text{no} corresponds to absence of such a witness.
4. Field-carried claim invariants (§7). authBound : α ≤ v.auth and inTheta : v.id ∈ ctx.caps are stored, so Claim carries its own evidence of well-formedness.
5. Global ledger with transitive taint (§8). DepStar is Relation.TransGen; Lemma 8.4 (transitive hold) and Theorem 8.6 (pre-compromise survival) are proved against a Ledger.
6. Corrected Promotion guard (§8.4). A claim is held when a dependency is held; the v10.0 direction was reversed.

1.4 Non-Claims

We do not claim truth. We do not claim elimination of hallucination. We do not report empirical results. The module below is core Lean 4 only; Mathlib is not imported. Mechanization coverage is given in §13 and is deliberately narrower than the prose in every case where the repo does not yet contain a proof.

