2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Dependent Claim Types

Definition 2.1 (Verified claim, informal). A verified claim over T : \mathrm{Prop} is a tuple \langle T, p, w, \alpha, \kappa, \sigma, \pi, \tau, \phi \rangle together with an accepted-checker set cs such that every c \in cs accepts p.\text{bytes} at T.

The formal definition (Definition 4.2) makes the checker set explicit, so that soundness of p at T is derivable only from a named hypothesis about some c \in cs.

2.2 Trust Boundary

The proof-checking TCB is

\mathrm{TCB}_\text{proof} \;=\; \bigwedge \begin{cases} \text{Lean kernel} \\ \text{Lean4Lean} \\ \text{nanoda\_lib @ pinned commit} \\ \text{multi-checker export agreement} \\ \text{axiom whitelist \texttt{permitted\_axioms}} \end{cases}

Remark 2.2 (Residual export-format risk). No checker is assumed sound. nanoda_lib trusts the exporter to emit a well-formed .export file; the export format is part of the TCB. Prior art that motivates this framing:

· FLT repository (Anthropic) double-checks each proof with a Comparator binding the proof to a reference statement, plus nanoda on a lean4export file, under a permitted_axioms list.
· nanocomparator ports Lean's Comparator to Rust on top of nanoda; it formalises statement-binding, which is exactly the mechanism V-Core's Reform (§6) abstracts.
· unsorry uses two independent autoformalizations, normalises and diffs them, and flags disagreements; its known weakness is correlated formalizer error, which we name as Assumption 6.4.
· con-leche reports checkers accepting export streams the official kernel rejects on metadata fields; this motivates the multi-checker TCB in §2.2.

Correction 2.3. v10.0 cited "NIST SP 1276 v3 (2024)". The correct reference for the beacon construction used here is NISTIR 8213 draft v2 (2019). We cite the draft we rely on and do not assert that a v3 has been published.

2.3 Freshness vs. Unforgeability

\mathrm{NF\text{-}Remote} = \mathrm{EUF\text{-}CMA} \mid \mathrm{key\_integrity}, \qquad
\mathrm{Freshness} = \mathrm{beacon\text{-}combine} \mid \exists i.\, \mathrm{Beacon}_i\ \text{honest}

NISTIR 8213 draft v2 combination with precommitment:

A[T{-}1].\mathrm{precommit} = \mathrm{SHA512}(A[T].\mathrm{localRandom})

Z \leftarrow \mathrm{SHA512}\bigl(A[T{-}1].\mathrm{output} \parallel B[T{-}1].\mathrm{output} \parallel A[T].\mathrm{localRandom} \parallel B[T].\mathrm{localRandom}\bigr)

The beacon provides freshness, not unforgeability.

