3. Authority Poset

3.1 Sub-Posets

\mathcal{A}_\mathrm{formal} = \{\texttt{declared} \prec \texttt{derived} \prec \texttt{verified}\},\quad
\mathcal{A}_\mathrm{emp} = \{\texttt{untested} \prec \texttt{observed} \prec \texttt{replicated}\},\quad
\mathcal{A}_\mathrm{repro} = \{\texttt{unverified} \prec \texttt{traceable} \prec \texttt{reproducible}\}

Each is a finite total chain; encode via toNat.

3.2 Product Order

\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_\mathrm{formal} \times \mathcal{A}_\mathrm{emp} \times \mathcal{A}_\mathrm{repro}, \qquad
(a_1, b_1, c_1) \preceq (a_2, b_2, c_2) \iff a_1 \le a_2 \wedge b_1 \le b_2 \wedge c_1 \le c_2

Theorem 3.1 (Partial order, mechanized). (\mathcal{A}, \preceq) is a partial order.

Proof. Reflexivity and transitivity are componentwise Nat.le_refl and Nat.le_trans. For antisymmetry, apply the injectivity lemmas for toNat componentwise:

```lean
theorem Authority.le_antisymm {a b : Authority} (h1 : a ≤ b) (h2 : b ≤ a) : a = b := by
  have hf := Formal.toNat_inj (Nat.le_antisymm h1.1 h2.1)
  have he := Empirical.toNat_inj (Nat.le_antisymm h1.2.1 h2.2.1)
  have hr := Repro.toNat_inj (Nat.le_antisymm h1.2.2 h2.2.2)
  cases a; cases b; simp_all
```

\square

Theorem 3.2 (Join, mechanized). Authority.sup is a least upper bound: a \le a \sqcup b, b \le a \sqcup b, and a \le c \wedge b \le c \Rightarrow a \sqcup b \le c. The proof is componentwise on the finite chains.

3.3 Well-Formedness of the Product

Definition 3.3 (WF). An authority a is well formed, a \in \mathrm{WF}, iff empirical support of at least observed requires provenance of at least traceable:

a.\text{emp} \geq \texttt{observed} \;\Rightarrow\; a.\text{repro} \geq \texttt{traceable}

Observation 3.4 (Orthogonality, corrected). A tautology \top is formal-maximal and empirical-undefined: it is not a claim in the empirical sub-poset. A falsifiable prediction is empirical-maximal and formal-minimal. v10.0 said "empirical-maximal" of a tautology; the corrected statement is that orthogonality is a structural property of the well-formed product, not of the bare product.

