4. Checkers: The TCB Made Explicit

Definition 4.1 (Checker). A checker is an untrusted-by-default relation

c : \langle \text{name} : \mathrm{String},\ \text{accepts} : \mathrm{String} \to \mathrm{Prop} \to \mathrm{Prop} \rangle.

Its soundness is a separate hypothesis:

\mathrm{Sound}(c) := \forall\, \text{bytes} : \mathrm{String},\ \forall\, T : \mathrm{Prop},\ c.\mathrm{accepts}(\text{bytes}, T) \to T.

Definition 4.2 (Proof). For a non-empty list cs : \mathrm{List}\,\mathrm{Checker} and T : \mathrm{Prop},

\mathrm{Proof}(cs, T) := \bigl\langle \text{bytes} : \mathrm{String},\ \text{meta} : \mathrm{Meta},\ \text{witnessHash} : \mathrm{String},\ \text{nonempty} : cs \ne [],\ \text{accepted} : \forall c \in cs.\, c.\mathrm{accepts}(\text{bytes}, T) \bigr\rangle.

Theorem 4.3 (Diversity, mechanized). One sound checker in the accepted set suffices:

\exists c \in cs.\ \mathrm{Sound}(c) \;\Rightarrow\; \forall p : \mathrm{Proof}(cs, T).\ T.

```lean
theorem Proof.sound {cs : List Checker} {T : Prop} (p : Proof cs T)
    (hs : ∃ c, c ∈ cs ∧ Sound c) : T :=
  let ⟨c, hc, hsound⟩ := hs
  hsound p.bytes T (p.accepted c hc)
```

Corollary 4.4 (No false proofs, mechanized).

\bigl(\exists c \in cs.\ \mathrm{Sound}(c)\bigr) \Rightarrow \neg\,\mathrm{Nonempty}\bigl(\mathrm{Proof}(cs, \mathrm{False})\bigr).

Definition 4.5 (Concrete compatibility). For a hash function H : \mathrm{String} \to \mathrm{String},

\mathrm{Compat}(H, p, w) := p.\mathrm{meta} = w.\mathrm{meta} \wedge p.\mathrm{witnessHash} = H\,(w.\mathrm{artifact}).

Remark 4.6. v10.0's K_α was string equality with no hash function, making Lemma 4.4 vacuous. The current Compat compares a meta record (data, not a tautology) and an explicit hash of the artifact. Any assumption on H (collision resistance) is external and named, not silently assumed.

