5. Verifiers and No-Escalation

5.1 Lineage Structure

A nested inductive Verifier-contains-Verifier is rejected by Lean's kernel, so ancestry is a lineage (nearest parent first) with a proof that authority is non-increasing along it.

Definition 5.1 (Descends).

\mathrm{Descends}(a, [\,]) := \top, \qquad
\mathrm{Descends}(a, p :: ps) := a \le p \wedge \mathrm{Descends}(p, ps).

Lemma 5.2 (Mechanized). If \mathrm{Descends}(a, L) and x \in L, then a \le x.

```lean
theorem Descends.mem_le : ∀ (l : List Authority) (a : Authority),
    Descends a l → ∀ x, x ∈ l → a ≤ x
  | [], _, _, x, hx => by cases hx
  | p :: ps, a, ⟨hap, hrest⟩, x, hx => by
      cases hx with
      | head => exact hap
      | tail _ hx' => exact Authority.le_trans hap (Descends.mem_le ps p hrest x hx')
```

5.2 Verifier

Definition 5.3 (Verifier). With a private constructor,

\mathrm{Verifier} := \langle \mathrm{id} : \mathbb{N},\ \mathrm{auth} : \mathcal{A},\ \mathrm{lineage} : \mathrm{List}\,\mathcal{A},\ \mathrm{descends} : \mathrm{Descends}(\mathrm{auth}, \mathrm{lineage}) \rangle.

Assumption 5.4 (Sole constructor). The only ways to obtain a Verifier are

\texttt{Genesis.root}(g, \mathrm{id}, a, h) \quad\text{and}\quad \texttt{Verifier.issue}(p, \mathrm{id}, a, h : a \le p.\mathrm{auth}).

This module-boundary discipline is the single residual architectural assumption.

Definition 5.5 (Issuance).

\texttt{issue}(p, \mathrm{id}, a, h) := \langle \mathrm{id},\ a,\ p.\mathrm{auth} :: p.\mathrm{lineage},\ \langle h, p.\mathrm{descends} \rangle \rangle.

Theorem 5.6 (No-escalation, mechanized). Every verifier's authority is bounded by every ancestor's authority:

\forall v\, \forall x \in v.\mathrm{lineage}.\ v.\mathrm{auth} \le x.

```lean
theorem no_escalation (v : Verifier) : ∀ x, x ∈ v.lineage → v.auth ≤ x :=
  Descends.mem_le v.lineage v.auth v.descends
```

Corollary 5.7. A newly issued verifier is bounded by its parent:

(p.\texttt{issue}\ \mathrm{id}\ a\ h).\mathrm{auth} \le p.\mathrm{auth}.

Correction 5.8. v10.0 wrote h_\text{no-escalation} : \alpha_i \preceq \alpha_{i+1}, which admits escalation. The correct hypothesis is a \le p.\mathrm{auth} at each issuance step, and the induction on Descends closes over arbitrary finite chains.

