6. Faithfulness

Definition 6.1 (Reform). A reformalisation of an informal statement P at a proposition T is

\mathrm{Reform}(P, T) := \langle \mathrm{formalizerId} : \mathbb{N},\ T' : \mathrm{Prop},\ \mathrm{equiv} : T \leftrightarrow T' \rangle.

Definition 6.2 (\equiv_\mathrm{reform}). Two propositions T, T' are \equiv_\mathrm{reform}-equivalent iff there exists a definitional isomorphism T \leftrightarrow T' after normalisation. Formally:

T \equiv_\mathrm{reform} T' \;:=\; \exists\, e : T \leftrightarrow T'.\ \mathrm{Normalises}(e).

Design note. v10.0 defined \equiv_\mathrm{reform} by definitional equality; this is not decidable in general and excludes provably-equivalent but not-definitionally-equal reformalisations. Version 11.0 uses provable equivalence; the checker-supplied equiv : T ↔ T' field is the mechanism.

Definition 6.3 (Faithfulness predicate).

\phi(c) = \texttt{yes} \;\iff\; \exists\, r : \mathrm{Reform}(P, T_c).\ r.\mathrm{formalizerId} \ne \mathrm{producer}(c).

The negative branch is the absence of such a witness.

Assumption 6.4 (Formalizer independence). Prior art (unsorry) documents that two formalisations by correlated formalizers may share errors. V-Core names this as an assumption: \mathrm{Reform} requires independent formalizers, and any guarantee that two formalizers are independent is external.

Remark 6.5 (Comparator connection). nanocomparator binds a proof to a reference statement, which is the formal half of \mathrm{Reform}. V-Core treats statement binding as a checker property (§4) and the informal-to-formal mapping as external data (§6), keeping the two separate.

