8. Revocation–Promotion Interaction

8.1 Ledger and Effective-Time Revocation

Definition 8.1 (Ledger).

\mathrm{Ledger} := \langle \mathrm{deps} : \mathbb{N} \to \mathrm{List}\,\mathbb{N},\ \mathrm{signer} : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N},\ \mathrm{sealedAt} : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N} \rangle,

where \mathrm{deps}(c) lists the claims that c depends on.

Definition 8.2 (Revocations). \mathrm{Rev} := \mathbb{N} \to \mathrm{Option}\,\mathbb{N}; \mathrm{rev}(v) = \mathrm{some}\ t means verifier v is revoked with effective time t.

Definition 8.3 (HOLD, inductive).

\frac{\mathrm{rev}(L.\mathrm{signer}(c)) = \mathrm{some}\ t \quad t \le L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c)}{\mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, c)} \tag{H-direct}

\frac{d \in L.\mathrm{deps}(c) \quad \mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, d)}{\mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, c)} \tag{H-taint}

Definition 8.4 (DepStar).

\mathrm{DepStar}(L, c, d) := \mathrm{Relation.TransGen}\bigl(\lambda c\, d.\ d \in L.\mathrm{deps}(c)\bigr)(c, d).

8.2 Key Lemma

Lemma 8.5 (Transitive freeze, mechanized). If c transitively depends on a held d, then c is held:

\mathrm{DepStar}(L, c, d) \wedge \mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, d) \;\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, c).

```lean
theorem hold_of_depStar {L : Ledger} {rev : Revocations} {c d : Nat}
    (h : DepStar L c d) (hd : Hold L rev d) : Hold L rev c := by
  revert hd
  induction h with
  | single hcd => exact fun hd => Hold.taint hcd hd
  | tail _ hbc ih => exact fun hd => ih (Hold.taint hbc hd)
```

Correction 8.6. v10.0's Dependents ranged over claims with the same T, \alpha, \kappa, \sigma, \pi, \tau, \phi, excluding dependents with a different proposition, and used String ∈ String. The ledger formulation above is heterogeneous and transitive.

8.3 Effective-Time Behaviour

Theorem 8.7 (Pre-compromise survival, mechanized). If a claim was sealed strictly before its signer's effective compromise time, it is not directly held:

L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c) < t \;\wedge\; \mathrm{rev}(L.\mathrm{signer}(c)) = \mathrm{some}\ t \;\Rightarrow\; \neg\bigl(t \le L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c)\bigr).

```lean
theorem pre_compromise_not_direct {L : Ledger} {rev : Revocations} {c t : Nat}
    (_hr : rev (L.signer c) = some t) (hbefore : L.sealedAt c < t) :
    ¬ (rev (L.signer c) = some t ∧ t ≤ L.sealedAt c) := by
  intro ⟨_, h⟩; omega
```

Pre-compromise claims may still be tainted through a dependency; the theorem only rules out the direct revocation clause.

8.4 Promotion

Definition 8.8 (Promotion).

\frac{
\alpha \le \alpha' \quad \alpha' \le c.\mathrm{verifier}.\mathrm{auth} \quad \neg\,\mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, c.\mathrm{cid})
}{
c : \mathrm{Claim}(cs, T, \alpha) \;\longmapsto\; c' : \mathrm{Claim}(cs, T, \alpha')
}

Theorem 8.9 (Correct guard, mechanized). A claim with a held dependency is itself held, so promotion cannot fire:

d \in L.\mathrm{deps}(c) \wedge \mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, d) \;\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{HOLD}(L, \mathrm{rev}, c).

```lean
theorem held_dependency_blocks {L : Ledger} {rev : Revocations} {c d : Nat}
    (hdep : d ∈ L.deps c) (hd : Hold L rev d) : Hold L rev c := Hold.taint hdep hd
```

Correction 8.10. v10.0's Promotion rule blocked a claim c if a dependent of c was revoked. This is the wrong direction: revocation of a dependent does not taint its dependency. The corrected guard blocks c when a dependency is held. Theorem 7.6 of v10.0 is withdrawn.

Remark 8.11 (Regime stability). v10.0's regimeStable as "no revocation anywhere" is over-broad; it would freeze the entire ledger on any revocation. Version 11.0 replaces it with the explicit HOLD-based premise: a promotion is admissible iff c is not held under the current ledger and revocation map.

