14. Conclusion

Version 11.0 corrects the mechanization–prose gap in v10.0 and narrows the coverage claim to what the repository proves. The irreducible human dependencies are:

1. The constitutional stipulation of (\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}, \mu) and of the genesis verifier set (§10).
2. The external formalizer that supplies Reform P T (§6, Assumption 6.4).
3. The sole-constructor discipline on Verifier (Assumption 5.4), enforced by module boundaries rather than the kernel.
4. The soundness hypothesis on at least one checker in cs (Theorem 4.3), never an implicit axiom.

Under those assumptions, V-Core produces typed, scoped, provenance-aware authority with: field-carried claim invariants that survive a public constructor, no-escalation over arbitrary finite issuance lineages, effective-time revocation with transitive taint, and a corrected promotion guard. It does not produce truth; it produces claims whose authority is bounded by every ancestor and whose soundness is conditional on a named checker being sound.

Appendices to V-Core v11.0

The following appendices expand V-Core v11.0 with full Lean source, proofs, architecture, protocol specifications, design rationale, and open obligations. Notation: \mathcal{A} denotes the authority poset; L the ledger; \mathrm{rev} the effective-time revocation map; \mathrm{cs} a non-empty checker list; H : \mathrm{String} \to \mathrm{String} a hash function.

