Appendix B — Full Proofs with Commentary

B.1 Authority Poset

Lemma B.1.1 (Injectivity of toNat). For each sub-poset X \in \{\mathrm{Formal}, \mathrm{Empirical}, \mathrm{Repro}\}, x.\mathrm{toNat} = y.\mathrm{toNat} \Rightarrow x = y.

Proof. By exhaustive case analysis on the finite enumeration. In Lean, cases x <;> cases y <;> simp_all [X.toNat] closes all nine goals (three values each). \square

Theorem B.1.2 (Partial order). (\mathcal{A}, \le) is a partial order.

Reflexivity. Componentwise Nat.le_refl.
Transitivity. Componentwise Nat.le_trans.
Antisymmetry. Suppose a \le b and b \le a. Then a.\mathrm{formal}.\mathrm{toNat} \le b.\mathrm{formal}.\mathrm{toNat} and b.\mathrm{formal}.\mathrm{toNat} \le a.\mathrm{formal}.\mathrm{toNat}, so by Nat.le_antisymm the toNats are equal, and by Lemma B.1.1 the Formal values are equal. Repeat for empirical and repro, then cases a; cases b; simp_all. \square

Theorem B.1.3 (Lub). a.sup b is the least upper bound of a, b.

Proof. Componentwise on the three finite chains. For each component X, X.sup a b = if a.toNat ≤ b.toNat then b else a. Then:

· a \le a \sqcup b: if a.toNat ≤ b.toNat the value is b.toNat and a.toNat ≤ b.toNat holds; otherwise the value is a.toNat and Nat.le_refl closes.
· b \le a \sqcup b: symmetric.
· a \le c \wedge b \le c \Rightarrow a \sqcup b \le c: in each case, the if branch's value is either a.toNat or b.toNat, both \le c.\mathrm{toNat}.

Formally in Lean, cases ha : a.formal <;> cases hb : b.formal <;> simp [...] closes each goal. \square

B.2 Checker Framework

Theorem B.2.1 (Diversity). If \exists c \in cs.\ \mathrm{Sound}(c), then for any p : \mathrm{Proof}(cs, T), we have T.

Proof. Destructure the hypothesis to obtain c, hc : c \in cs, hs : \mathrm{Sound}(c). Then p.\mathrm{accepted}\ c\ hc : c.\mathrm{accepts}(p.\mathrm{bytes}, T), and hs\ p.\mathrm{bytes}\ T applied to that yields T. \square

Corollary B.2.2 (No false proof). Under the same hypothesis, \neg\,\mathrm{Nonempty}(\mathrm{Proof}(cs, \mathrm{False})).

Proof. Immediate from B.2.1 with T := \mathrm{False}. \square

Design note. The theorem is diversity-monotone: adding more checkers to cs cannot weaken the soundness guarantee (the existential ∃ c is not weakened), and can only strengthen it. This is what motivates the multi-checker TCB: an attacker must corrupt every sound checker simultaneously.

B.3 Lineage and No-Escalation

Lemma B.3.1 (Descends.mem_le). If \mathrm{Descends}(a, L) and x \in L, then a \le x.

Proof. Structural induction on L.

· L = [\,]: x \in [\,] is impossible; cases hx closes the goal.
· L = p :: ps: x \in p :: ps gives x = p or x \in ps.
  · x = p: Descends(a, p :: ps) gives a \le p, i.e. a \le x.
  · x \in ps: Descends(a, p :: ps) gives a \le p and Descends(p, ps); induction gives p \le x; transitivity gives a \le x. \square

Theorem B.3.2 (No-escalation). For any verifier v, v.\mathrm{auth} \le x for every x in v.\mathrm{lineage}.

Proof. Immediate from B.3.1 with a := v.\mathrm{auth} and L := v.\mathrm{lineage}, using v.\mathrm{descends}. \square

Theorem B.3.3 (Issuance bound). For any parent p, child c = p.\texttt{issue}\ \mathrm{id}\ a\ h, c.\mathrm{auth} \le p.\mathrm{auth}.

Proof. Unfold Verifier.issue: the new auth is a, and the hypothesis is h : a ≤ p.auth. \square

Theorem B.3.4 (Full ancestor bound). Any issued verifier's authority is bounded by every ancestor's authority.

Proof. For c = p.\texttt{issue}\ \mathrm{id}\ a\ h, the lineage of c is p.\mathrm{auth} :: p.\mathrm{lineage}. For x in this list:

· x = p.\mathrm{auth}: apply B.3.3.
· x \in p.\mathrm{lineage}: apply B.3.3 then B.3.2 for p.

Both are instances of Authority.le_trans h (no_escalation p x hx). \square

Remark B.3.5 (Correction to v10.0). v10.0 stated \alpha_i \preceq \alpha_{i+1} as the issuance hypothesis, which permitted escalation. The correct hypothesis, a \le p.\mathrm{auth}, is embedded in the type of Verifier.issue. The induction on Descends closes over arbitrary finite chains, because Descends is a List-structural predicate, not a fixed-length one.

B.4 Claim Invariants

Theorem B.4.1 (Field-carried bound). Any c : \mathrm{Claim}(cs, T, \alpha) satisfies \alpha \le c.\mathrm{verifier}.\mathrm{auth} and c.\mathrm{verifier}.\mathrm{id} \in c.\mathrm{ctx}.\mathrm{caps}.

Proof. These are exactly the fields authBound and inTheta. Because Verifier and Claim both use private mk ::, no other construction path exists outside the module; but even if there were, the fields are part of the type, so the invariant holds for any value of the type. \square

Theorem B.4.2 (Claim soundness). If some checker in cs is sound, then for any c : \mathrm{Claim}(cs, T, \alpha), we have T.

Proof. c.proof : Proof cs T, and Theorem B.2.1 applies. \square

Theorem B.4.3 (Ancestor bound). Any c : \mathrm{Claim}(cs, T, \alpha) satisfies \alpha \le x for every x \in c.\mathrm{verifier}.\mathrm{lineage}.

Proof. Combine c.authBound : α ≤ c.verifier.auth with no_escalation c.verifier x hx : c.verifier.auth ≤ x. \square

Corollary B.4.4. A claim sealed at authority \alpha cannot be promoted above the authority of any ancestor of its verifier, even with all promotion predicates satisfied.

B.5 Ledger and HOLD

Definition B.5.1 (HOLD, structural). Hold L rev c is the least predicate closed under:

· (H-direct) \mathrm{rev}(L.\mathrm{signer}(c)) = \mathrm{some}\ t \wedge t \le L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c) \Rightarrow \mathrm{HOLD}(c);
· (H-taint) d \in L.\mathrm{deps}(c) \wedge \mathrm{HOLD}(d) \Rightarrow \mathrm{HOLD}(c).

Lemma B.5.2 (Monotonicity in the dependency graph). If DepStar L c d and Hold L rev d, then Hold L rev c.

Proof. Induction on the TransGen derivation.

· Base (single hcd): d \in L.\mathrm{deps}(c), apply H-taint.
· Step (tail _ hbc ih): the derivation has c \to^{*} b \to d with DepStar L b d and d ∈ L.deps c. By induction hypothesis, Hold L rev b from Hold L rev d; apply H-taint with d ∈ L.deps c and Hold L rev b to obtain Hold L rev c. \square

Theorem B.5.3 (Pre-compromise survival). If L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c) < t and \mathrm{rev}(L.\mathrm{signer}(c)) = \mathrm{some}\ t, then the H-direct clause cannot fire.

Proof. H-direct requires t \le L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c), contradicting L.\mathrm{sealedAt}(c) < t. \square

Remark B.5.4 (Taint still applies). Pre-compromise survival concerns only the H-direct clause. A pre-compromise claim may still be held via H-taint if one of its dependencies is held.

B.6 Promotion

Theorem B.6.1 (Correct guard). A claim with a held dependency is itself held.

Proof. Apply H-taint to hdep : d ∈ L.deps c and hd : Hold L rev d. \square

Correction B.6.2. v10.0 blocked a claim when a dependent was revoked. This is the wrong direction: revocation of a dependent does not taint its dependency, because dependencies flow from a claim to its prerequisites, not vice versa. The direction is enforced in the module by an explicit example:

```lean
example : ∃ (L : Ledger) (rev : Revocations),
    Hold L rev 1 ∧ ¬ Hold L rev 0
```

where claim 1 depends on claim 0 and the signer of 1 is compromised. Claim 1 is held; claim 0 is not. This witness would fail under v10.0's reversed rule.

