Appendix E — Threat Model Elaboration

E.1 Adversary Classes

Class Description V-Core layer
\mathcal{A}_1 External network attacker L4
\mathcal{A}_2 Compromised host/runtime L3
\mathcal{A}_3 Malicious runner L2
\mathcal{A}_4 Compromised verifier key L1
\mathcal{A}_5 Corrupted genesis Constitutional
\mathcal{A}_6 Correlated formalizer error Meta

E.2 Attack Trees

E.2.1 Forge a claim at authority \alpha. Attacker must:

1. Produce Proof cs T accepted by every c \in cs, or
2. Produce a Claim with authBound : α ≤ v.auth for some legitimate v, or
3. Break the private-constructor discipline.

Path (1): requires breaking every sound checker, mitigated by Theorem B.2.1 (diversity) and Annex G.
Path (2): requires an unchecked Verifier, blocked by private mk :: and Assumption 5.4.
Path (3): a module-boundary attack; the only residual architectural risk.

E.2.2 Bypass revocation. Attacker must:

1. Show that a claim's signer was not revoked, or
2. Show that the claim predates the effective time, or
3. Show that no dependency was held.

Path (1): if the signer is revoked with effective time t and the claim was sealed at \ge t, H-direct fires.
Path (2): the claim survives only if it is genuinely pre-compromise.
Path (3): H-taint fires transitively (Theorem B.5.2).

E.2.3 Launder a false proposition through reformalization. Attacker must defeat \phi(c) = \mathrm{yes}, i.e., obtain a Reform P T with a false T. Path blocked by Assumption 6.4 (independence) and by the external-checker discipline: the producing system cannot supply the Reform.

E.3 Residual Risks (Unmitigated by V-Core Itself)

· Correlated formalizer error. If the independent formalizer shares a misconception with the producer, \phi = \mathrm{yes} can be wrong. This is outside the mechanized fragment.
· Export-format defects. The multi-checker TCB reduces but does not eliminate this risk; see Annex G.
· Genesis capture. If the constitutional quorum is compromised, no theorem in Appendix B helps.

