Appendix F — Design Rationale and Rejected Alternatives

F.1 Why Checker-Parameterised Proofs, Not an Uninterpreted TCB

Alternative A. axiom check : String → Prop → Bool and axiom check_sound : check b T = true → T.

Rejected. An axiom is invisible to a reader and unbounded in scope. Parameterising Proof by an explicit List Checker makes the TCB a first-class object: which checkers are trusted is a value in the module, and any theorem consuming a proof names the specific checker it trusts.

F.2 Why Lineage, Not Nested Verifier

Alternative B. inductive Verifier where | mk : Verifier → Authority → Verifier.

Rejected. Lean's kernel rejects non-uniform nested inductives of this shape. The lineage encoding is equivalent for the no-escalation theorem and avoids the kernel limitation.

F.3 Why Reform as Data, Not a Predicate

Alternative C. def Faithful (P : String) (T : Prop) : Prop := ....

Rejected. A predicate without a witness is unfalsifiable and cannot be mechanized. Reform as a structure forces the producer to supply an actual T ↔ T' proof and a formalizer identity, which is a checkable artifact.

F.4 Why a Global Ledger, Not Per-Claim Dependents

Alternative D. def Dependents {T α κ σ π τ φ} (c : Claim T α κ σ π τ φ) : Set (...) := { d | c.proofRef ∈ d.proofRef ∨ ... }.

Rejected. Forces every dependent to have the same type parameters as c, excludes cross-proposition dependencies, and uses String ∈ String which is not a membership relation. The ledger formulation (deps : Nat → List Nat) is heterogeneous and transitive.

F.5 Why Effective-Time Revocation

Alternative E. Boolean revocation: revoked : Nat → Bool.

Rejected. Boolean revocation retroactively invalidates pre-compromise claims, which is too strong. An effective time t allows pre-compromise claims to survive unless tainted through a dependency.

F.6 Why the Promotion Guard Is on Dependencies

Alternative F. Block c if a dependent of c is revoked.

Rejected. Deps point from a claim to its prerequisites, not from a prerequisite to its dependents. Revoking a dependent does not affect the truth of the dependency; only the reverse direction matters. The corrected rule is Theorem B.6.1.

